    {
      "module": "module.aws_migrator[0]",
      "mode": "managed",
      "type": "sg_runbook_sop",
      "name": "aws_discovery_pr_review",
      "provider": "provider[\"releases.stackgen.com/stackgen/stackgen\"]",
      "instances": [
        {
          "schema_version": 0,
          "attributes": {
            "approve": true,
            "description": "# AWS discovery PR review\n\nUse this skill while preparing the discovery PR and final handoff. Treat it as a review lens, not a template for conclusions.\n\nRead the generated artifacts and decide what a reviewer needs to know. Ground statements in the account, region, scan settings, state, Cloud2Code log, per-type summaries, Terraform groups, and validation outputs. Connect each notable finding to its source and its practical effect on review or downstream migration.\n\nUse specific observations instead of generic praise or workflow narration. Keep counts tied to the artifact that produced them. A warning sample does not describe every skipped resource. If the logs do not identify a cause, leave it unknown. Avoid repeating metrics that do not change the review, and do not claim fixes, coverage, or validation that the artifacts do not show.\n\nThe PR should let a human quickly see what was discovered, what needs attention, where the evidence lives, and what remains uncertain. Make the judgment from the evidence; this skill does not prescribe a completeness verdict.",
            "id": "7ed1be95-ddcf-5835-a7e3-99d9be5ed751",
            "name": "aws-discovery-pr-review",
            "status": "approved",
            "version": 1
          },
          "sensitive_attributes": [],
          "dependencies": [
            "data.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.gcp",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "data.sg_remote_runner.customer",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_secret.aws_vault",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_secret.github_vault",
            "terraform_data.agent_stack_prerequisites",
            "terraform_data.governance_codify_prerequisites"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "module": "module.aws_migrator[0]",
      "mode": "managed",
      "type": "sg_runbook_sop",
      "name": "aws_migrator_orchestration",
      "provider": "provider[\"releases.stackgen.com/stackgen/stackgen\"]",
      "instances": [
        {
          "schema_version": 0,
          "attributes": {
            "approve": true,
            "description": "# AWS Cloud2Code to Terraform reverse-IaC orchestration\n\nScan one AWS region, generate readable Terraform files, and make sure they pass\nTerraform format and validation checks. Attempt a zero-change plan as an\noptional stronger check. Then open a pull request with readiness suggestions.\n\n## Phases\n\n0. **Runner preflight** — verify remote-runner tools (`jq`, `aws`, `python3`, `git`, `tofu`/`terraform`, `cloud2code`) and script-pack preload before any scan or ingest work. Terminal blockers jump to the final gate via `conditional_skip`.\n1. **AWS scan** — capture one region as a monolithic Terraform state.\n2. **Decomposition** — split the monolith into logical, layered Terraform roots.\n3. **Reverse-IaC scaffold** — write HCL-only roots with import blocks and open the AWS IaC pull request.\n4. **Terraform validation** — generate, format, validate, optionally test and lint, and attempt a zero-change plan.\n5. **Azure generation** — build a migration blueprint, generate Azure Terraform roots, validate them, loop until they pass, and open the Azure pull request.\n6. **Orphan handoff** — hand any resources that cannot be hydrated safely to the secondary orphan-module procedure; skip cleanly when there are none.\n7. **Final gate** — confirm every phase produced its evidence before reporting complete (or emit a single blocked rollup when an upstream gate short-circuited).\n\n## Execution host\n\nA single execution host owns all shell work: `cloud2code`, `aws`, `jq`, `git`,\n`tofu`/`terraform`, optional `tflint`, and state file operations. Batch\nmulti-step shell work into one sequence rather than many rapid one-off commands,\nand keep independent work in bounded parallel batches. All heavy parsing and\nTerraform runs happen on the host, never inline.\n\nRemote `execute_series` has a multi-second Guild dispatch floor (claim + return),\noften ~5–7s even for a trivial command. Prefer batching listings with the work\nthat needs them. Efficient listing patterns:\n\n`groups/*/generated.tf`, or\n`find \"$WORK_ROOT/groups\" -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 2 -name generated.tf`, or\n`sample_group_ids.json`.\n\nAvoid unconstrained `find` from `$WORK_ROOT` when the plugin cache lives under\nthat tree. Prefer `/bin/bash -c` over `bash -lc` when inventing one-liners.\n\nPack sanitizers rewrite `route = [ {…} ]` into `route { }` blocks and move aside\nexisting `generated.tf` before `-generate-config-out`. Prefer re-running the\nconverge pack after surgical edits over wiping whole group files.\n\nWhen diagnosing slow probes, fold on-host timing into the next real series:\n\n```bash\n/bin/bash -c 'd=\"$WORK_ROOT\"\necho \"onhost_start=$(date +%s%3N)\"\nTIMEFORMAT=\"onhost_find_groups_sec=%R\"\ntime find \"$d/groups\" -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 2 -name generated.tf | wc -l\nTIMEFORMAT=\"onhost_find_all_sec=%R\"\ntime find \"$d\" -name generated.tf | wc -l\necho \"onhost_end=$(date +%s%3N)\"'\n```\n\nIf on-host times are under ~0.2s while Guild still reports multi-second wall\ntime, the cost is dispatch, not CPU. Do not resize the runner for probe latency.\n\n## Handoff record\n\nCarry these facts forward between phases (paths, counts, statuses, and redacted\nsummaries only — never secrets or raw state attribute payloads):\n\n| Key | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `cloud2code_scan_ok` | Scan completed and produced a valid tfstate |\n| `cloud2code_tfstate_path` / `monolith_state_uri` | State file path on the host |\n| `monolith_resource_count` | Managed resource instance count in the monolith |\n| `logical_group_manifest` | Decomposition manifest path |\n| `group_state_paths` | Per-group state paths |\n| `count_reconciliation_ok` | Aggregate group count matches monolith count |\n| `split_quality_status` | Whether optional grouping-readiness analysis was generated |\n| `registry_mapping_report` | Reverse-IaC registry/scaffold report |\n| `orphans_bundle` | Addresses that cannot be hydrated into normal HCL roots |\n| `hcl_hydration_status:\u003cgroup_id\u003e` | Per-group validation status JSON |\n| `terraform_validation_ok` | True when all selected Terraform folders pass required format and validation checks |\n| `multi_plan_zero_diff_ok` | Optional stronger result: all selected folders also have a zero-change plan |\n| `azure_migration_blueprint_path` | Azure migration blueprint JSON path |\n| `azure_review_needed_path` | Azure review-needed markdown path |\n| `azure_iac_generated` | True when `azure/groups/\u003cgroup_id\u003e/` roots were written |\n| `azure_iac_validation_ok` | True when Azure static validation passed and the live plan passed or was skipped for missing credentials |\n| `azure_plan_status` | `success`, `success:sample:N/M`, `skipped:missing_credentials` (only when require_azure_live_plan is off), or `failed` |\n| `azure_pr_url` / `pr_url` | Destination Azure PR URL |\n\n## AWS scan\n\nThe scan requires `aws_region`. Optional inputs include `cloud2code_include`,\n`cloud2code_exclude`, `cloud2code_tags`, `cloud2code_output_dir`,\n`cloud2code_discovery_name`, `iac_repository_url`, `default_branch`, grouping\ncontrols, and `tfstate_decomposer_*` tuning controls. Always force\n`--auto-import=false`. Record the scan result and the monolith state URI.\n\n## Decomposition\n\nConsume `monolith_state_uri`; never ask the operator for state paths. Using the\ndecomposition script pack:\n\n1. Download or copy the monolith state onto the host under the run working directory.\n2. Run the state decomposer.\n3. Verify every scanned resource appears exactly once and every group has a state file.\n4. When readiness analysis is available, use it to improve grouping.\n5. Continue with the correct split even when the optional readiness report or tuning history is missing.\n\n### Readiness analysis\n\nThe grouping score, tuning history, orphan count, and review queue are optional.\nUse them to suggest improvements, but do not stop Terraform generation because\nthey are missing or below a score threshold. Stop only when resources are\nmissing, duplicated, or do not have generated group state files.\n\nLarge states hydrate **every** group by default so each folder gets a\n`generated.tf`. Set `DBSPLIT_HYDRATE_SAMPLE_SIZE` only as an emergency\nwall-time / disk cap; when sampling is forced, mark final evidence as partial.\nFmt/validate failures are readiness signals (`terraform_validation_ok=false`,\nreport **needs work**) — they must not abort discovery or skip pushing HCL.\n\n## Reverse-IaC scaffold\n\nScaffold HCL-only Terraform roots under `aws/groups/\u003cgroup_id\u003e/`, write import\nblocks, sync artifacts to the target IaC repository, and open or prepare a pull\nrequest. All committed Terraform configuration must be HCL `.tf`; state JSON,\nplan JSON, and parser scratch files stay under the run working directory.\n\n## Terraform validation loop\n\nFor each selected group root, run:\n\n1. `tofu init -backend=false -input=false -no-color`\n2. `tofu plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf -input=false -lock=false -no-color`\n3. `tofu fmt -recursive -check -no-color`\n4. `tofu validate -no-color`\n5. `tofu test -no-color` when `*.tftest.hcl` or `*.tftest.json` files exist\n6. `tflint` when installed on the host\n7. `tofu plan -refresh=false -input=false -lock=false -no-color -out=verify.tfplan`\n\nProvider install failures and host disk exhaustion are repairable init failures.\nMove `TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR` and `TF_DATA_DIR` to a scratch volume when available,\nremove only Terraform runtime/cache artifacts (never `terraform.tfstate` or\nsource artifacts), retry init, and record `hcl_init_status:\u003cgroup_id\u003e` with the\ninit log path and classified reason.\n\nRecord `hcl_hydration_status:\u003cgroup_id\u003e` with:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"generated_tf_path\": \".../generated.tf\",\n  \"plan_no_changes\": true,\n  \"remaining_actions\": { \"add\": 0, \"change\": 0, \"destroy\": 0 },\n  \"validation\": {\n    \"fmt\": \"true\",\n    \"validate\": \"true\",\n    \"test\": \"skipped:no_tests\",\n    \"lint\": \"skipped:tflint_missing\"\n  },\n  \"attempt\": 1\n}\n```\n\nLoop the validation phase until `terraform_validation_ok` is `\"true\"`, a\nreal runner problem is recorded, or `4` is reached. Keep pushing\nhydrated `generated.tf` to the PR on every pass — validation false must not\nskip sync or end the run early.\n\n## Azure generation\n\nAzure generation starts only after AWS convergence, uses the default Azure\nmigration profile, and never asks for human approval of service choices.\n\nThe blueprint step writes:\n\n- `azure/artifacts/migration-profile.json`\n- `azure/artifacts/migration-blueprint.json`\n- `azure/artifacts/review-needed.md`\n\nGeneration writes HCL-only Terraform roots under `azure/groups/\u003cgroup_id\u003e/`.\nPreferred demo mappings are EKS to AKS, Auto Scaling Group or launch template to\nVMSS, EC2 singleton to VM, Lambda to Azure Functions, S3 to Storage Account,\nRDS/PostgreSQL to Azure Database for PostgreSQL Flexible Server, SQS to Service\nBus Queue, and SNS/EventBridge/Kinesis to Event Grid or Event Hubs. Ambiguous,\nlow-confidence, identity, security, or placeholder mappings are recorded in the\nreview-needed artifacts and the pull request body.\n\nValidation runs `tofu fmt`, `tofu validate`, optional `tofu test`, optional\n`tflint`, and a live `tofu plan` only when Azure credentials are configured on\nthe host. If credentials are absent and live plan is not required, set `azure_plan_status=skipped:missing_credentials`; when required, fail validate.\nthat is acceptable when static validation passes. If a live plan runs, success\nrequires expected creates and no deletes or replacements. Post-apply zero-diff\nis out of scope for this demo. Loop Azure/GCP generation until\n`*_iac_validation_ok` is conclusive (`\"true\"` or `\"false\"`) or a terminal\nblocker is recorded — destination generate is deterministic, so a false\nvalidate must exit the loop and open the PR rather than thrash to the visit\ncap. Cap is `4` iterations.\n\nThe Azure pull request syncs the generated `azure/` tree into the target repo\nunder `azure/`, allocates a fresh branch starting with `azure/\u003crun id\u003e`, and\nopens a new pull request. If that branch already exists or has any PR history,\nappend a timestamp/PID suffix instead of reusing or updating the old PR. The PR\nbody must include a summary, generated group count, validation status, plan\nstatus, mapping decisions, unsupported/placeholder resources, and review-needed\nitems.\n\n## Orphans\n\nAddresses that cannot be read, imported, or represented safely in generated HCL\nmove to `orphans_bundle` with a reason and source group. The orphan handoff\nskips cleanly when the bundle is empty; otherwise it hands the bundle to the\nsecondary orphan-module procedure.\n\n## Final gate\n\nFor AWS discovery, prefer reporting **needs work** (not failed) when the PR has\nhydrated HCL but `terraform_validation_ok` is still false — include remaining\n`hcl_fix_target` lines. Treat as complete/ready when:\n\n- Scan evidence exists.\n- Monolith and aggregate group counts match.\n- Required HCL hydration records exist (and preferably validate green).\n- `pr_url` / `iac_pr_url` exists, or a concrete PR blocker is recorded.\n- The orphan handoff is skipped-empty or recorded.\n\nDestination Azure/GCP gates apply only to those workflows.\n\n## Blockers\n\nStop with a compact blocked reason on missing AWS region, missing host tools,\nmissing source state, invalid state, script-pack verification failure,\nmissing or duplicated resources, or missing Terraform. Soft validation /\nzero-diff failures are not terminal blockers once hydrate is on the PR.",
            "id": "22513943-5682-51fc-8a30-9fe066885115",
            "name": "aws-migrator-orchestration-sop",
            "status": "approved",
            "version": 6
          },
          "sensitive_attributes": [],
          "dependencies": [
            "data.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.gcp",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "data.sg_remote_runner.customer",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_secret.aws_vault",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_secret.github_vault",
            "terraform_data.agent_stack_prerequisites",
            "terraform_data.governance_codify_prerequisites"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "module": "module.aws_migrator[0]",
      "mode": "managed",
      "type": "sg_runbook_sop",
      "name": "azure_demo_migration_profile",
      "provider": "provider[\"releases.stackgen.com/stackgen/stackgen\"]",
      "instances": [
        {
          "schema_version": 0,
          "attributes": {
            "approve": true,
            "description": "# Azure review-candidate migration profile\n\nThis runbook is the default, versioned migration profile for Azure generation.\nOutput is **review-candidate IaC** — safe to open a PR and inspect — not apply-ready landing zones.\n\n## Operating mode\n\n- Best effort, no approval gates for ambiguous mappings.\n- Do not ask clarifying questions for service mapping choices.\n- Continue with a reasonable Azure default when the source intent is ambiguous.\n- Record every ambiguous, low-confidence, security-sensitive, or unsupported mapping in `azure/artifacts/review-needed.md` and the PR body.\n- If a strong equivalent does not exist, generate the closest valid Terraform scaffold or a clearly marked placeholder and document why review is needed.\n- Treat catalog `status=mapped` as \"target type known\". Trust `emission` for what HCL was actually written (`full_scaffold` vs `managed_identity_scaffold` vs `resource_group_only`).\n\n## Open standards (realistic floor)\n\n- **Microsoft CAF naming** abbreviations (`rg-`, `vnet-`, `snet-`, `nsg-`, `st`, `id-`, `vmss-`, `aks-`, ...).\n- **Azure Well-Architected security defaults**: TLS 1.2+, HTTPS-only storage, private containers, SSH-only Linux compute (no password defaults in git).\n- **OpenTofu / HashiCorp root layout** per group (`versions.tf`, `providers.tf`, `variables.tf`, `main.tf`, `outputs.tf`) plus `azure/.gitignore` excluding plans/logs/state.\n- **Not** full Azure Verified Modules (AVM) / CAF landing-zone composition for every AWS service — that remains an operator follow-up.\n\n## Mapping source of truth (do not invent mappings)\n\n- The AWS Terraform type to Azure Terraform type mapping is defined by the versioned catalog `scripts/mappings/aws-to-azure.json` (preloaded with the script pack). The `azure-migration-blueprint` and `azure-iac-generate` stages resolve it deterministically via `scripts/azure_mapping_catalog.py`; you do not hand-map resource types.\n- **Do not invent an alternate Azure service when a catalog default exists.** Use the catalog `default_target_resource_type`. Only propose an alternate from that type's `target_resource_types` list, and record the reason in `review-needed.md`.\n- Types marked `non_applicable` (e.g. IAM users, parameter groups, OAI) fold into Entra/other resources or have no Azure resource. Never scaffold a fake service for them; list them as review/skipped.\n- Types absent from the catalog resolve as `unsupported`: the generated root is a resource group plus a review note. Prefer adding the type to the catalog (see `mappings/README.md`) over one-off mappings in a PR.\n\n## Default mappings (aligned to the catalog)\n\nThe table below is a human-readable summary of `scripts/mappings/aws-to-azure.json`; the catalog is authoritative when they differ.\n\n| AWS source | Azure default | Review focus |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| EKS (`aws_eks_cluster`) | AKS (`azurerm_kubernetes_cluster`) | Networking, RBAC, node pools, ingress, add-ons |\n| Auto Scaling Group / launch template | VMSS (`azurerm_linux_virtual_machine_scale_set`) | Image, user data, scaling policy, load balancer |\n| EC2 singleton (`aws_instance`) | Linux VM (`azurerm_linux_virtual_machine`) | OS image, SSH key, disks, network interfaces |\n| Lambda (`aws_lambda_function`) | Azure Functions (`azurerm_linux_function_app` + plan + storage) | Runtime, triggers, packaging, environment, identity |\n| S3 (`aws_s3_bucket`) | Storage Account (`azurerm_storage_account`) + container companion | Naming, containers, lifecycle, encryption, public access |\n| RDS/PostgreSQL (`aws_rds_cluster` / `aws_db_instance`) | Azure Database for PostgreSQL Flexible Server | Version, HA, extensions, backup, firewall, Key Vault password |\n| ElastiCache | Azure Cache for Redis | SKU, TLS, private endpoint, auth |\n| Route53 zone/record | Azure DNS zone / record | Public vs private, cutover |\n| CloudWatch log group | Log Analytics workspace | Retention and diagnostic settings |\n| ECS cluster/task | Container Apps environment (+ review) | Whether AKS is a better fit |\n| CloudFront | Front Door profile (review-heavy) | Origins, WAF, custom domains |\n| SQS (`aws_sqs_queue`) | Service Bus Queue (`azurerm_servicebus_queue`); Storage Queue alternate | FIFO/dead-letter semantics, retry behavior |\n| IAM role/policy | Managed identity scaffold + RBAC review notes | Emission is identity-only; operators author RBAC |\n| IAM user/group/access key | Entra / non_applicable | Do not recreate static cloud users in azurerm |\n\n## Defaults\n\n- Location: `eastus`\n- Resource group pattern: `rg-\u003cgroup_id\u003e`\n- Tags: `generated_by=stackgen-aws-migrator`, `migration_mode=review-candidate`, `standards=caf-waf-opentofu`, `migration_group=\u003cgroup_id\u003e`\n- Networking: VNet/subnet/NSG with deny-Internet inbound baseline when compute/network categories are present; CIDRs are variables for IPAM replacement.\n- Identity: user-assigned managed identity scaffold; IAM→RBAC stays review-needed.\n- Compute access: SSH public key variable (placeholder must be replaced before apply); password auth disabled.\n- SKU posture: low-cost review SKUs unless the source group strongly implies otherwise.\n- Module source preference: local Terraform roots under `azure/groups/\u003cgroup_id\u003e`; replace with AVM/CAF modules later if desired.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Static: `tofu fmt`, `tofu validate` on **all** generated groups (unless an explicit sample override is set).\n- Live plan: sampled when `AZURE_LIVE_PLAN_MAX_GROUPS` is set; PR must disclose `planned/total`.\n- Never apply migrated Azure resources from this workflow.\n- When live plan is required, missing credentials fail validation.",
            "id": "51c18309-2f13-5cab-87a6-9f3fc0259ca1",
            "name": "aws-migrator-azure-migration-profile-sop",
            "status": "approved",
            "version": 1
          },
          "sensitive_attributes": [],
          "dependencies": [
            "data.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.gcp",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "data.sg_remote_runner.customer",
