      "name": "mapping_catalog_knowledge",
      "provider": "provider[\"releases.stackgen.com/stackgen/stackgen\"]",
      "instances": [
        {
          "schema_version": 0,
          "attributes": {
            "approve": true,
            "description": "# Discovery mapping review reference\n\nThis is an index to the live-in-repository migration sources, not another mapping authority. Read the selected destination catalog and resolver for the decision used in a run.\n\n- Azure: `mappings/aws-to-azure.json`, resolved by `azure_mapping_catalog.py`; emitted HCL comes from `azure_iac_generate.py`.\n- GCP: `mappings/aws-to-gcp.json`, resolved by `gcp_mapping_catalog.py`; emitted HCL comes from `gcp_iac_generate.py`.\n- The catalogs record version, source type, target type, category, confidence, review note, companions, and any mapped attributes. Resolver output also derives status, emission, and HITL lane. The generator determines actual output.\n- A target name or attribute mapping is a candidate decision, not proof of semantic equivalence. Check the source state values, generator output, and target provider schema for required fields and nested block shape.\n- A `mapped` status does not by itself mean full-equivalent HCL. Check emission (`full_scaffold`, profile/identity scaffold, `resource_group_only`, `none`, or another value) and the review note.\n- For AWS type semantics and attributes, use the AWS provider schema/docs; for target arguments, use the selected AzureRM or Google provider version schema/docs. Record which source/version supports a claim if it affects an edit.\n- Keep corrections at the owning layer: catalog/resolver for a bad decision, generator for repeated wrong HCL, run HCL for run-specific values, and provider/version only for a confirmed schema issue. Keep AWS observations separate from assumed destination values.\n\nExamples from the current catalogs to investigate, not copy as universal rules:\n\n- `aws_vpc.cidr_block` is mapped toward a target network address-space field. Check whether the source value needs conversion to the target collection shape.\n- `aws_subnet.vpc_id` is mapped toward a target network reference. Confirm whether the target expects a name, self-link, or generated-resource reference rather than copying an AWS ID.\n- `aws_db_instance` defaults to a PostgreSQL target in both catalogs, while its review note calls out engine selection. Check the source engine before accepting that default.\n- `aws_iam_role` has no ordinary attribute map. The generators emit identity scaffolding; they do not automatically translate AWS actions to Azure RBAC or GCP IAM.\n- `aws_s3_bucket.bucket` maps toward a target storage name. Check provider constraints and whether generation emits an account/bucket/container structure.\n\nFor exhaustive per-type decisions and current values, inspect the versioned JSON catalogs. Do not copy or create a second static mapping table here.",
            "id": "edc888ba-3f0d-5eb4-b989-c75ae71e7c4a",
            "name": "aws-migrator-mapping-catalog-reference",
            "status": "approved",
            "version": 1
          },
          "sensitive_attributes": [],
          "dependencies": [
            "data.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.gcp",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "data.sg_remote_runner.customer",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_secret.aws_vault",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_secret.github_vault",
            "terraform_data.agent_stack_prerequisites",
            "terraform_data.governance_codify_prerequisites"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "module": "module.aws_migrator[0]",
      "mode": "managed",
      "type": "sg_runbook_sop",
      "name": "mapping_provider_schema_reasoning",
      "provider": "provider[\"releases.stackgen.com/stackgen/stackgen\"]",
      "instances": [
        {
          "schema_version": 0,
          "attributes": {
            "approve": true,
            "description": "# Mapping catalog and provider-schema research\n\nUse this when deciding or reviewing an AWS-to-Azure/GCP mapping or generated target HCL. Existing catalogs, resolvers, and emitters are authoritative; this note is an index and method, not another mapping table.\n\n- Azure: `mappings/aws-to-azure.json` → `azure_mapping_catalog.py` → `azure_iac_generate.py`.\n- GCP: `mappings/aws-to-gcp.json` → `gcp_mapping_catalog.py` → `gcp_iac_generate.py`.\n\nRead the decision for the source type: status, target, category, confidence, emission, review note, companions, and attribute mapping. Then inspect the emitter to see what it actually writes. `mapped` means a catalog target exists, not that equivalent HCL is complete. `non_applicable`, `unsupported`, `none`, placeholder, profile, and identity outputs differ.\n\n## Research a mapping instead of guessing\n\nDo not stop at the checked-in catalog when a source type is unsupported, low-confidence, or only receives a placeholder/profile scaffold. The catalog is not an exhaustive answer; treat it as a prior, not a closed list. Use the actual source state to understand what the AWS object does, then search the **Terraform Registry** for both provider resources and reusable community/official modules; inspect each candidate's documentation at the version pinned by the generated root (`\u003ccloud\u003e/groups/\u003cgroup_id\u003e/versions.tf`). Provider resources and modules are different choices: prefer a maintained Terraform module when it composes the required native resources correctly and fits this repo's local-root/runner constraints; inspect its source, inputs, outputs, version, provider requirements, and license before adopting. A module result is not evidence by itself—compare the expanded resources and schema against the AWS instance. Start with Registry provider/module search and exact version docs, for example:\n\n- Terraform Registry provider/resource search: `https://registry.terraform.io/search/providers?q=\u003cservice-or-resource-keyword\u003e`\n- Terraform Registry module search: `https://registry.terraform.io/search/modules?q=\u003cservice-or-architecture-keyword\u003e`\n- Google provider resource/data-source docs: `https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/google/\u003cpinned-version\u003e/docs/resources/\u003cresource-name\u003e` or `/docs/data-sources/\u003cname\u003e`.\n- AzureRM provider resource/data-source docs: `https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/azurerm/\u003cpinned-version\u003e/docs/resources/\u003cresource-name\u003e` or `/docs/data-sources/\u003cname\u003e`.\n\nSearch by the AWS service concept and observed behavior, not just by `aws_*` name. Inspect required arguments, nested blocks, lifecycle/identity model, networking, supported fields, references, and whether the candidate is a resource or data source. If Registry search or a page is unavailable, use the official provider documentation/source for the same pinned release and record that source; do not infer schema from an unpinned “latest” page. Compare against AWS provider docs/state and relevant GCP service semantics. A similar name or a successful `tofu validate` is not evidence of behavioral equivalence.\n\nFor each gap, form and compare plausible target candidates. Record the source type/instance, candidate resource or module address/version, pinned provider/module versions, Registry/upstream URLs, observed source attributes, semantic fit and mismatches, confidence, and unresolved decisions in the cloud run's `*/artifacts/mapping-research.md` (or inline evidence in the mapping decision). Then choose the most defensible candidate when evidence supports it; use modules only when their composition is reviewable, version-pinned, compatible with local roots, and does not hide per-instance wiring. Explicitly retain unresolved/unsupported disposition when evidence is insufficient. Never fabricate source values or claim parity from resource counts alone.\n\n## Turn research into a correct change\n\nA per-run decision may update the run's blueprint and generated HCL only when the target is supported by cited evidence and the generator can express the required shape. For a repeatable AWS type/category gap, update the AWS→GCP catalog and generator in the script-pack source, add a fixture from representative AWS state plus assertions on emitted HCL and source-to-target accounting, bump the catalog/pack versions, and run the GCP tests. Do not “fix” a missing mapping by simply relabeling it `mapped` or reusing an unrelated category. Keep source evidence, Registry evidence, assumptions, confidence, and residual gaps distinguishable for reviewers.\n\nFix the owning layer: catalog/resolver for a bad decision, generator for repeatable output, run blueprint/HCL for a run-specific evidence-backed choice, or provider config for a confirmed schema issue. If schema evidence is unavailable, leave the claim unverified. Add a focused regression test when changing reusable behavior.",
            "id": "e18b5d6b-3d53-578a-9245-6e6945791a75",
            "name": "mapping-provider-schema-reasoning",
            "status": "approved",
            "version": 1
          },
          "sensitive_attributes": [],
          "dependencies": [
            "data.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.gcp",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "data.sg_remote_runner.customer",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_secret.aws_vault",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_secret.github_vault",
            "terraform_data.agent_stack_prerequisites",
            "terraform_data.governance_codify_prerequisites"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "module": "module.aws_migrator[0]",
      "mode": "managed",
      "type": "sg_runbook_sop",
      "name": "nile_governance_learn_and_conform",
      "provider": "provider[\"releases.stackgen.com/stackgen/stackgen\"]",
      "instances": [
        {
          "schema_version": 0,
          "attributes": {
            "approve": true,
            "description": "# Nile governance — learn on the fly, then conform\n\nThis SOP is a **method**, not a control catalog. Nile markdown in Governance-and-Policy keeps changing. Each `azure-iac-governance-conform` / `gcp-iac-governance-conform` visit must **refresh** the current docs, **rebuild** a per-resource decision tree from that text, **author** a validator from the tree, **execute** it against generated IaC, **fix** gaps (pack + agent), and **re-verify**. Do **not** reuse a prior run's tree or a frozen Priority-1 list in Python.\n\nValidation evidence is **not** human approval (Evidence Schema / PRR Gate 11).\n\n## Authority this run\n\n1. Refresh into `$WORK_ROOT/governance/` (harness `--refresh`). Fail closed on fetch failure (`blocked:governance_docs_unavailable`).\n2. Record repo, ref, commit SHA, and paths in `azure|gcp/artifacts/governance-source.json`.\n3. Optional human pin: Nile-Factory submodule `docs/nile-governance` — **not** the runtime source unless fetch is impossible (still fail closed).\n4. When refreshed docs contradict older artifacts in the same PR, **current docs win**. Note the delta in `governance-decision-tree.json`.\n\nRead (under the refreshed tree):\n\n- `governance/policy-catalog.md`\n- `governance/control-matrix.md`\n- `governance/tagging-labeling-standard.md`\n- `governance/priority-controls.md`\n- `governance/production-readiness-gates.md`\n- `governance/evidence-schema.md`\n- exception / approval models when present\n- `architecture/` overviews when present\n\n## Rebuild the decision tree (every visit)\n\nDo **not** reuse last run's tree when:\n\n- `governance-source.json` SHA ≠ last recorded SHA\n- inventory has new resource types\n- findings cite a control that current docs renamed or removed\n\nFor each inventoried resource R with type T:\n\n1. Map T → category from **current** matrix/catalog text (compute, storage, networking, identity, data, serverless, other). Inventory `suggested_category` is a hint only.\n2. Walk applicable **Priority-1** leaves from current `priority-controls.md` + catalog (metadata/tags, identity, network, data protection). Logging / backup / privileged access that cannot be decided from HCL → `evidence_gap` (does not invent topology).\n3. Candidate / deferred controls are **not** active unless current docs say so.\n4. Do **not** invent controls absent from this-run docs.\n\nWrite `azure|gcp/artifacts/governance-decision-tree.json` with `governance_commit_sha`, type→category, and leaves used.\n\n## Author and run the validator\n\nReplace the harness scaffold `azure|gcp/artifacts/governance-validator.py` (must drop `NILE_GOVERNANCE_VALIDATOR_SCAFFOLD`). Implement **this-run** tree only.\n\nEmit `azure|gcp/artifacts/governance-findings.json` with per finding: `resource_address`, `decision_path`, `control_id`, `nile_priority`, `blocks_commit`, `autofixed`. Then run:\n\n```text\npython3 $WORK_ROOT/scripts/governance_conform.py --work-root $WORK_ROOT --cloud azure|gcp --run-validator\n```\n\nOr re-run the stage harness (`run-destination-stage.sh azure-iac-governance-conform` or `gcp-iac-governance-conform`).\n\nThe harness runs **OPA Rego** from Nile-Factory `rules/` against each group's `tofu plan` JSON. Each policy pack owns both its deny predicate and structured, resource-specific remediation direction (`data.policy.remediation`); the checker only transports that guidance to `governance-opa-guidance.json` and fix hints. **Python does not patch generated HCL.** Read `failure_classes` in `governance-opa-report.json`, `governance-opa-guidance.json`, `governance-opa-fix-hints.md`, and `governance-opa-findings.json`. For provider-schema/plan failures, diagnose the exact failure and edit the generator, catalog, or HCL as appropriate; never let a generic fixer guess.\n\nwhen `*_iac_governance_ok` is false after the pack pass.\n\n## Remediate and iterate (agent owns residuals)\n\nFor each residual Rego direction / deny:\n\n1. Prefer values from AWS inventory / source tags / blueprint metadata.\n2. If the source has no value, **make a migration assumption**: pick a Nile-safe placeholder (not TAG-003 banned tokens like `tbd`/`test`/`unknown`), write the HCL or `variables.tf` / `*.auto.tfvars` change, and append a row to `azure|gcp/artifacts/governance-assumptions.md` (what, why, how to replace for prod).\n3. Allowed edits: labels, tags, var defaults, provider required args, `name_prefix`, private-by-default / TLS flags named by denies.\n4. Re-run the execute series after edits.\n\nFor a plan failure, use `failure_classes` and the full diagnostic before editing. Find the generator/catalog/policy source that produced the failure, make the durable correction when you own that layer, add a focused regression test, regenerate or fix the generated HCL, and rerun validation. A repair that does not change the next report requires a new diagnosis—not repeating the same edit. Distinguish provider schema errors, missing credentials, invalid values/dependencies, and OPA policy defects; do not mislabel an unavailable plan as an IaC pass.\n\n### Reason from the cloud resource model, not generic Terraform vocabulary\n\nFor each deny, trace the chain **source evidence → intended target behavior → destination resource type → provider-supported HCL/plan field → policy field lookup**. Do not assume a field named `labels` exists, or that every resource exposes metadata at the same path. Confirm the target provider's schema/documentation and the resource's effective planned value (including nested blocks) before editing. If valid HCL puts the value in a provider-specific location but the policy only inspects a generic field, classify it as a policy/schema mismatch and fix the policy plus a plan-shaped regression test; do not distort correct IaC to satisfy a bad predicate. If the field is truly absent from HCL and supported by the provider, fix the generator or generated HCL at the right layer. Preserve the distinction between verified source values and migration assumptions.\n\nAfter every repair, compare the new structured findings and the exact plan/evaluation input with the previous iteration. If the same denies remain, or the evidence fingerprint is unchanged, stop automatic retries and explain why (repair did not reach the evaluator, stale plan, policy path mismatch, unsupported field, or no semantic change). Never report a fix as applied merely because a write was attempted; prove it changed HCL and then prove the evaluator saw the changed value. A timeout, missing harness, empty inventory, missing governance SHA, or conflicting validator/OPA result is **inconclusive/blocked**, not a clean conformance report.\n\nDo **not** invent IAM action translations or network redesigns.\n\nLoop until every resource is Priority-1-conformant **and** OPA reports `opa_ok=true` (`*_iac_governance_ok=true`), or until max iterations / a terminal blocker. Prefer clearing residuals before the destination PR stage. If residuals remain, the PR still opens with TODO + assumptions — do not abandon without a PR. Pin the governance SHA in artifacts and the PR body.\n\n## Forbidden\n\n- Freezing policy text into Nile-Factory as the control catalog\n- Treating candidate GCP controls as active unless current docs say so\n- Claiming `governance-findings.json` satisfies human approval / Gate 11\n- Leaving the run with empty `pr_blocker` and no destination PR when only governance/OPA/validate residuals remain (document them in TODO.md + assumptions instead)\n- Accepting “silly” incomplete failures without attempting a concrete HCL/var substitution",
            "id": "e3ff4ce2-0da8-5980-be87-7002facbd96f",
            "name": "nile-governance-learn-and-conform-sop",
            "status": "approved",
            "version": 5
          },
          "sensitive_attributes": [],
          "dependencies": [
            "data.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.gcp",
            "data.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "data.sg_remote_runner.customer",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_guild_integration.aws",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.aws_integration.sg_secret.aws_vault",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_guild_integration.github",
            "module.aws_migrator.module.github_integration.sg_secret.github_vault",
            "terraform_data.agent_stack_prerequisites",
            "terraform_data.governance_codify_prerequisites"
          ]
        }
      ]
