    {
      "module": "module.aws_migrator[0]",
      "mode": "managed",
      "type": "sg_runbook_sop",
      "name": "terraform_state_shard_extraction",
      "provider": "provider[\"releases.stackgen.com/stackgen/stackgen\"]",
      "instances": [
        {
          "schema_version": 0,
          "attributes": {
            "approve": true,
            "description": "# Logical grouping of a monolithic Terraform/OpenTofu state\n\nSplit a monolithic Terraform/OpenTofu state into logical groups for **AWS,\nAzure, GCP** (and mixed) — not limited to RDS.\n\nKeywords: terraform show -json, state list, jq, dependency closure, connectivity, connected components, tags, default_tags, module path, for_each, workspace, aws_*, azurerm_*, google_*, azapi_*, logical_group, shard.\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Monolith state JSON or `terraform state list` output (path recorded as **`monolith_state_local_path`** — often under **`/tmp`** when the primary workdir is read-only).\n- Optional **`grouping_policy_json`**: rules such as\n  - **Tag keys** (e.g. `app`, `service`, `team`, `cost-center`) — match `values.tags` / provider `default_tags` in state.\n  - **Module address prefix** (e.g. `module.data_plane`).\n  - **Resource type regex** (e.g. `^(aws_db_|azurerm_mssql_)`).\n  - **Explicit include/exclude lists** of addresses.\n- Optional **`grouping_strategy`** string:\n  - Omitted or **`tfstate_monolith_decomposer`** — primary path. Run **`tfstate_monolith_decomposer.py split`**, which classifies resources into Foundation (L1), Platform (L2), and Application (L3), records cross-layer dependencies in **`upstream_refs.json`** (not merge signals), and emits per-group state shards. Hydrate inlines literal attribute IDs in `generated.tf`; do **not** emit `terraform_remote_state` / `upstream.tf` scaffolds.\n  - **`layered_three_tier`** — alias for the same decomposer algorithm.\n  - Legacy values **`policy_first`**, **`tag_seeded_connectivity`**, **`tag_seeded_connectivity_capped`**, **`connectivity`**, **`connectivity_capped`**, and **`type_chunk`** remain accepted for old payloads, but this AWS-to-Azure workflow should normalize back to **`tfstate_monolith_decomposer`** unless an operator explicitly directs otherwise.\n- Optional **`max_resources_per_appstack`** — hard ceiling on **managed resource instance count** per `group_id` in **`logical_group_manifest`** after allocation. With `tfstate_monolith_decomposer`, this is a candidate knob for deterministic cap partitioning; `0` means unlimited and is often best when cap-splitting creates cross-group references.\n- Optional decomposer tuning controls:\n  - **`tfstate_decomposer_env_scope`** — `all` (default), `l2l3`, or `l2`; changes which layers are partitioned by environment.\n  - **`tfstate_decomposer_env_tag_keys`** — comma-separated environment tag keys; default `env,environment,Environment,stage,Stage`.\n  - **`tfstate_decomposer_layer3_tag_keys`** — comma-separated application tag keys; default `app,application,Application,service,team,project`.\n  - **`tfstate_decomposer_overrides_json`** / **`tfstate_decomposer_overrides_path`** — JSON with `resource_overrides`, `type_rules`, and `accept_provisional` for confirmed placement.\n  - **`tfstate_decomposer_layer_taxonomy_json`** — type-to-layer taxonomy written to `layer_taxonomy.json` before the split.\n  - **`tfstate_decomposer_skip_unknown_type_review`** — boolean; when true, unknown-type provisional placements are still scored instead of remaining review-blocking.\n  - **`tfstate_decomposer_max_tuning_iterations`** — bounded candidate count (default 10).\n\n## Steps (policy-first — default)\n\n1. **Normalize state**\n   Count resource **instances** (including `count`/`for_each` keys). Persist `monolith_resource_count`.\n\n2. **Anchor / seed detection (multi-vendor)**\n   Prefer **user grouping policy** when present. Otherwise infer **candidate anchors** from high-fanout types across clouds, for example:\n   - **AWS:** `aws_db_instance`, `aws_rds_cluster`, `aws_dynamodb_table`, `aws_elasticache_*`, `aws_opensearch_domain`, `aws_msk_cluster`, …\n   - **Azure:** `azurerm_mssql_server`, `azurerm_postgresql_flexible_server`, `azurerm_cosmosdb_account`, `azurerm_storage_account`, `azurerm_kubernetes_cluster`, …\n   - **GCP:** `google_sql_database_instance`, `google_bigquery_dataset`, `google_spanner_instance`, `google_redis_instance`, `google_container_cluster`, …\n   Emit `logical_group_seeds` (JSON): each seed `{address, resource_type, suggested_group_key}`.\n\n3. **Pattern-based clustering**\n   - **Tags:** build adjacency: resources sharing the same `(tag_key, tag_value)` for configured keys → same **logical_group**.\n   - **Module tree:** everything under `module.foo` shares group `foo` unless policy splits by submodule.\n   - **Provider split:** never put `aws_*` and `azurerm_*` in the same group; split on `provider[\"registry…\"]` boundaries when tags disagree.\n\n4. **Graph closure**\n   For each logical group, expand with dependency edges from state JSON so security groups, subnets, keys, and secrets **owned only by that group** ride along. Mark **shared** resources (NAT, org-wide KMS, hub VPC) as `shared_ambiguous` — assign to a dedicated `shared-services` group or exclude until a human rule; **never double-count**.\n\n5. **Manifest**\n   Output JSON **`logical_group_manifest`** (alias / successor to `shard_manifest`):\n   `group_id -\u003e { cloud_hint, resource_addresses[], notes? }`.\n   Also mirror legacy key **`shard_manifest`** with the same JSON if downstream steps still expect it.\n\n6. **Counts**\n   `per_group_resource_counts`, `aggregate_group_resource_count`; `count_reconciliation_ok` iff sum equals `monolith_resource_count` and no duplicate addresses.\n\n## 7. Connectivity-first grouping (`grouping_strategy`: `connectivity` or `connectivity_capped`)\n\nUse when operators want Terraform roots aligned to dependency structure instead of broad **resource-type** buckets (e.g. \"all IAM in one root\").\n\n1. **Build a resource graph** from `terraform show -json`: vertices = managed resource addresses (same `data.*` / deposed rules as policy mode). Add an **undirected** edge when either resource’s config references the other’s address (dependency edges, `depends_on` when visible in JSON, cross-resource attribute references).\n2. **Provider partition** — compute **connected components** **per cloud** (`aws_*` vs `azurerm_*` vs `google_*` / `azapi_*` never mixed in one `group_id`). Optional: within a giant component, merge only along **strong** edges (e.g. duplicate `depends_on` both ways) if telemetry shows hairball graphs — document the rule in `logical_group_manifest.notes`.\n3. **Shared hubs** — NAT gateways, org-wide KMS keys, default VPCs: assign to a dedicated `shared-*` `group_id` or keep in the largest attached component but **never double-count** addresses.\n4. **Optional policy blend** — if `grouping_policy_json` is present, use tag/module rules only to **subdivide** large components or **seed** partition roots, not to merge unrelated components across missing edges.\n5. **Manifest \u0026 counts** — emit **`logical_group_manifest`** / `shard_manifest` and `per_group_resource_counts` as in policy mode; use `group_id` names like `conn-aws-001` and `notes.grouping: connectivity`.\n\n## 8. Group size cap (`max_resources_per_appstack`)\n\nApply when **`max_resources_per_appstack`** is set (typically with **`connectivity_capped`**) or when operators cap **`policy_first`** results.\n\nLet **N** = positive integer parsed from the input (default **80** if `grouping_strategy` is `connectivity_capped` and the cap input is empty).\n\nAfter initial groups exist, **split** any `group_id` whose managed `resource_addresses` length **exceeds N**:\n\n1. Work on the **induced subgraph** of that group’s addresses.\n2. **Partition** into new `group_id`s each with **\u003c= N** addresses, best-effort **minimizing cut edges** (fewer cross-group references):\n   - Preferred: **seeded expansion** — pick a high-degree or policy-tagged seed, BFS/DFS add neighbors until N, start the next group from an unvisited high-degree node.\n   - Acceptable fallback: **deterministic bin-packing** (lexicographic order into chunks of N) when time-bounded; note `notes.partition: greedy-chunk` so plans can be revisited.\n3. If a **hub** resource would need to appear in multiple groups for correctness, keep it in **one** primary group and record dependent addresses that reference it in `notes.cross_shard_refs` / `upstream_refs.json`. Downstream hydrate keeps **literal IDs** in `generated.tf` (no `terraform_remote_state` lookup). Avoid duplicating the same address in two `group_id` lists.\n4. Assign fresh **`group_id`**s (suffix `-shard-01`, incrementing counter, etc.), recompute counts, and assert **every** group has `≤ N` resources and the global sum still equals `monolith_resource_count`.\n\n## 9. Per-group state shards (`groups/\u003cgroup_id\u003e/terraform.tfstate`)\n\nAfter **`split-manifest`** (or `extract-group-states`), each `group_id` has an isolated state file under **`groups/\u003cgroup_id\u003e/terraform.tfstate`**. These slices:\n\n- Contain only managed resource **instances** assigned to that group (including `count` / `for_each` keys).\n- Preserve monolith `version`, `terraform_version`, `serial`, and `lineage` metadata.\n- Use empty `outputs` in the shard (workload outputs stay in the monolith until migrated).\n\n**Scaffold and hydration steps** run `tofu plan` **inside each group directory** using its slice — do not re-parse the multi-megabyte monolith per group.\n\n## 9.5. Optional grouping-readiness analysis\n\n`split-manifest` is implemented by the script-pack file\n**`tfstate_monolith_decomposer.py`**. Run the decomposer and verify that every\nscanned resource appears exactly once. When the optional readiness report is\navailable, use it to improve grouping. A missing report must not stop Terraform\ngeneration or validation.\n\nPrimary command shape:\n\n```bash\npython3 tfstate_monolith_decomposer.py split \"$WORK_ROOT\" \"$WORK_ROOT/state/terraform.tfstate\" \"$CAP\" \\\n  --env-scope \"$ENV_SCOPE\" \\\n  --env-tag-keys \"$ENV_TAG_KEYS\" \\\n  --layer3-tag-keys \"$LAYER3_TAG_KEYS\" \\\n  --overrides \"$WORK_ROOT/overrides.json\" \\\n  --skip-unknown-type-review\npython3 tfstate_monolith_decomposer.py scaffold-registry \"$WORK_ROOT\"\n```\n\nOnly include optional flags when the corresponding input is non-empty.\n`tfstate_decomposer_layer_taxonomy_json` is written to\n`$WORK_ROOT/layer_taxonomy.json` before the split; the decomposer loads that file\nautomatically.\n\nTag seeds for partitioning are **generic** (app/service/team/… synonyms, case-\ninsensitive). There are no vendor-specific keys in the pack. Prefer\n`--layer3-tag-keys` / `--env-tag-keys` (or env `TFSTATE_DECOMPOSER_LAYER3_TAG_KEYS`,\n`TFSTATE_ALLOCATE_SEED_TAG_KEYS`) when the customer taxonomy differs; otherwise any\nnon-cloud-managed tag is used before falling back to resource type.\n\n### Deterministic sorting and partition order\n\nThe algorithm is intentionally deterministic so candidate outputs can be compared:\n\n- Unknown resource type prefix rules are checked in sorted layer order.\n- Resource graph connected-component traversal visits sorted vertices.\n- Small groups are merged by sorted seed keys and sorted resource addresses.\n- Cap splitting uses BFS seeded by highest degree, with ties by address; neighbors prefer same seed key, then higher degree, then address.\n- Type fallback chunking orders by `(type, address)`.\n- L1 components sort by size descending; L3 app buckets sort by app name.\n- Review items sort by descending impact score; batch type rules sort by type frequency descending.\n- Sample group IDs are sorted by `group_id`.\n\nA changed output should be explainable by changed inputs (`cap`, tag keys,\ntaxonomy, overrides, env scope, or unknown-type handling), not nondeterminism.\n\n### Quality scoring\n\nThe decomposer writes **`split_quality_report.json`** and\n**`split_tuning_history.json`** under `WORK_ROOT` and records their paths. The\nreport scores:\n\n- hard correctness: `count_reconciliation_ok`, duplicate addresses, unallocated addresses, missing groups, and cap violations;\n- orphan objective: `metrics.orphan_count` from `orphans_bundle.json` after registry scaffolding;\n- decomposition quality: group count, largest/smallest/average/median group sizes, singleton ratio, cross-shard refs, and shared refs;\n- review burden: high-impact review item count, batch-rule count, auto-assigned count, unknown/provisional placements, and stale overrides;\n- fallback smells: single oversized hairball, type-chunk fallback, over-fragmented singletons, and too many tiny groups.\n\nSelection priority is:\n\n1. Count reconciliation must be true and there must be no duplicate/unallocated addresses.\n2. Prefer the candidate with the highest quality score.\n3. On equal score, prefer the lower `metrics.orphan_count`.\n4. If still tied, prefer fewer high-impact review items and less fragmentation.\n\n### Rerun tuning controls\n\nWhen the report includes `recommendation`, rerun the decomposer with the\nrecommended controls, bounded by `tfstate_decomposer_max_tuning_iterations`.\nTypical optimizations:\n\n- High orphan count with `env_scope=all` → try `env_scope=l2l3`.\n- High orphan count with `env_scope=l2l3` → try `env_scope=l2`.\n- High-impact unknown-type review items → try `tfstate_decomposer_skip_unknown_type_review=true`, then encode durable fixes in `tfstate_decomposer_overrides_json` or `tfstate_decomposer_layer_taxonomy_json`.\n- Oversized group / hairball → try a positive `max_resources_per_appstack` such as `120`.\n- Cross-shard refs or singleton over-fragmentation after capping → try `max_resources_per_appstack=0`.\n- Incorrect environment or application grouping → rerun with explicit `tfstate_decomposer_env_tag_keys` or `tfstate_decomposer_layer3_tag_keys`.\n\nStop tuning when the report passes, a recommendation repeats, or the bounded\niteration limit is reached. Downstream steps must use the final correct\nmanifest even if readiness analysis failed. Copy any reports that exist into\nthe pull request and add plain-language suggestions for reports that do not.\n\n## 10. Shared hub isolation (`*-shared-*` groups)\n\nHigh fan-in resources (IAM roles/policies, KMS keys, shared log groups, S3\nbuckets referenced by many services) are pulled into **`{cloud}-shared-NNN`**\ngroups when in-degree ≥ threshold. Workload groups record **`shared_refs`** /\n**`cross_shard_refs`** in manifest notes and **`upstream_refs.json`**. Hydrate\ninlines literal IDs so each group root plans without remote-state lookups.\nHydrate **shared groups first**, then workload shards — this reduces plan drift\nfrom missing dependency targets.\n\n### Large states (speed + reliability)\n\n- **Do not** paste full state JSON into prose. Write `*.jq` / shell scripts to a file under the run working directory, then run short commands that reference the script **path**. Multi-step shell work is **always** batched into one sequence — never many rapid-succession one-off commands.\n- **Progressive passes:** instance count → provider/module histogram → manifest build, instead of one giant `jq` that materializes everything at once (avoids OOM, timeouts, and truncated output).\n- Keep each shell step under typical time limits; split by module prefix or by cloud if the monolith is huge.\n\n### Edge cases (addresses, deposed, data sources)\n\n- **Addresses** must match Terraform exactly, including **`count` / `for_each` keys** (e.g. `aws_instance.foo[0]`, `module.x.aws_s3_bucket.y[\"a\"]`). Stripping indices breaks allocation and count reconciliation.\n- **`deposed`** / tainted rows and **orphan** objects: decide include vs exclude per org policy; document in `logical_group_manifest.notes` so `monolith_resource_count` stays comparable to shard sums.\n- **`data.*`** entries in JSON snapshots are usually **not** managed resources — exclude from managed-instance totals unless policy says otherwise.",
